#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064002 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "44cf8c85-f228-5eaa-922d-613355cb7d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15070" :question/problem "Factor the quadratic expression $10a^2 + 11a + 3$." :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$10a^2 + 11a + 3$.\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10 \\times 3 = 30$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($11$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30$.\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n$(1,30), \\quad (2,15), \\quad (3,10), \\quad (5,6)$.\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,6)$ add to $11{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 + 6 &= 11✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5a + 6a$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10a^{2} + 11a + 3 &=  \\\\\n10a^{2} + 5a + 6a + 3 &=  \\\\\n(10a^{2} + 5a) + (6a + 3) &=  \\\\\n5a(2a + 1) + 3(2a + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (5a + 3)(2a + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d5115f8-05b7-5df5-92b1-b4faa95f8449" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Coefficients and Positive Constants"}] :question/id #uuid "ad032833-ecf8-5295-9a9f-f69f759c19b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-15072" :question/problem "Factor the expression $3x^2 - 16x + 16$." :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$3x^2 - 16x + 16$.\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3 \\times 16 = 48$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-16$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $48$.\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $48$ are\n$(-1,-48), \\quad (-2,-24), \\quad (-3,-16), \\quad (-4,-12), \\quad (-6,-8)$.\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-12)$ add to $-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-4) + (-12) &= -16✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4x - 12x$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 16x + 16 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 4x - 12x + 16 &=  \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 4x) - (12x - 16) &=  \\\\\nx(3x - 4) - 4(3x - 4) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(3x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cfe2121-66f0-53fa-b744-041fe830bf13" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Constants"}] :question/id #uuid "35bedd9f-bcb7-5024-bfe7-5ef6d34518bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-15073" :question/problem "Factor the quadratic expression $2c^2 + 15c - 8$." :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$2c^2 + 15c - 8$.\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times -8 = -16$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-16{:}$\n$(1,-16),\\quad (-1,16), \\quad (2,-8), \\quad (-2,8), \\quad (4,-4)$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15{:}$\n\n$$\n(-1) + 16 = 15 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $-c+16c$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c^{2} + 15c - 8 &=  \\\\\n2c^{2} - c + 16c - 8 &=  \\\\\n(2c^{2} - c) + (16c - 8) &=  \\\\\nc(2c - 1) + 8(2c - 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (2c - 1)(c + 8)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c1ee78-95df-5fc6-95f8-433525dfe80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w + 10)(2w + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061087} {:answer/id #uuid "c157e900-c997-57c3-9bc8-3c92e0771c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w + 5)(2w + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061088} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fe4cae-a096-5499-a502-ec9f4428d114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6w + 1)(w + 10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061089} {:answer/id #uuid "1cda3823-aa18-5095-be27-8eacf6726484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3w + 1)(w + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061090} {:answer/id #uuid "72d93fea-3212-5062-aa7a-7464c91bda2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w + 2)(2w + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061091}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b5de56ee-fdfa-5ee6-87f8-0226508bf6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061086}] :question/id #uuid "acf95ff0-c23c-5538-9064-3e039f763a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-111835" :question/problem "What is $6w^{2} + 19w + 10$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d5115f8-05b7-5df5-92b1-b4faa95f8449" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Coefficients and Positive Constants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d2c88bae-0e5d-514c-bf54-54c3a911e7e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061768}] :question/id #uuid "32c14ac4-d